Anticipatory bail is a pre‑emptive direction from a High Court or Court of Sessions that the accused will be released on bail if and when he is arrested. 
**Steps to obtain anticipatory bail**

- **Set out the material facts** – Explain why the accused is innocent or unlikely to tamper with evidence, note the absence of prior convictions (or only minor past cases) and any special circumstances such as the accused being a woman, child or sick person (Sridhar @ Sritharraj v. The Inspector Of Police (2024); Section 480, Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023). 
- **Attach a bail bond and sureties** – Courts commonly require the petitioner to execute a bond (e.g., Rs 10,000 with two sureties) and may condition the release on that bond (Praveen Kumar.T.P @ Praveen Panonneri S/O.M.T.Kunhiraman Nambiar v. State Of Kerala (2023)). 
- **Agree to conditions that the court may impose** – Typical conditions include: 
 * surrender to the investigating officer within a stipulated period (e.g., two weeks) (Pradeep Kumar C K v. State Of Kerala (2023); Haridas.B, v. State Of Kerala (2023)); 
 * appear before the magistrate on the date of surrender (Pradeep Kumar C K v. State Of Kerala (2023); Haridas.B, v. State Of Kerala (2023)); 
 * report to the investigating officer as and when required (Praveen Kumar.T.P @ Praveen Panonneri S/O.M.T.Kunhiraman Nambiar v. State Of Kerala (2023)); 
 * not intimidate or influence witnesses (Praveen Kumar.T.P @ Praveen Panonneri S/O.M.T.Kunhiraman Nambiar v. State Of Kerala (2023)); 
- **Rely on statutory provisions for bail** – Under **Section 480 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita** a person accused of a non‑bailable offence may be released on bail if “reasonable grounds” do not exist for a serious offence and the court is satisfied that “it is just and proper…for any other special reason” (Section 480, Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023). 
- **If the accused is indigent** – Section 478 of the same Act allows release on a bond without surety where the person cannot furnish sureties (Section 478, Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023). 
- **Special provision for children** – Where the accused is a child, Section 12 of the Juvenile Justice Act permits bail “with or without surety” unless release would endanger the child or defeat justice (Section 12, Juvenile Justice (Care and Protection of Children) Act, 2015). 

If the court is satisfied on the basis of the above facts and conditions, it will issue an anticipatory bail order directing that the accused be released on bail at the time of arrest, subject to the stipulated bond and any conditions imposed. If the petitioner fails to surrender as directed, the investigating officer may arrest him as if no order existed (Pradeep Kumar C K v. State Of Kerala (2023); Haridas.B, v. State Of Kerala (2023)).
